1 Preliminary information
*************************

The 'bbe' program is a sed-like editor for binary files.  It performs
basic byte-related transformations on blocks of the input stream.  'bbe'
is a non-interactive command-line tool and can be used as part of a
pipeline.  'bbe' makes only a single pass over the input stream.

   'bbe' also provides grep-like features, such as printing the
filename, offset, and block number.


File: bbe.info,  Node: Samples,  Next: Invoking bbe,  Prev: Overview,  Up: Top

2 Samples using 'bbe'
*********************

Users familiar with the standard stream editor 'sed' often encounter
similar data manipulation tasks on binary streams.  Below are practical
examples illustrating common 'bbe' operations, including binary
analogues to familiar 'sed' patterns:

'bbe -b "/\x80\x50\x0e/:12" -e "d 0 3" -e "c BCD ASC" -e "A \x0a" -e "w /tmp/numbers" -o /dev/null /tmp/bdata'
     Extract BCD-encoded numbers from the file '/tmp/bdata' and write
     them in ASCII format with a newline to '/tmp/numbers'.  The 12-byte
     blocks containing the BCD numbers start with the three-byte
     sequence '0x80', '0x50', and '0x0e'.  The first three bytes (the
     block start sequence) are removed ('d 0 3'), the rest of the block
     is transformed from BCD to ASCII ('c BCD ASC'), and a newline is
     appended at the end of the block ('A \x0a').  All transformed
     blocks are written to '/tmp/numbers' ('w /tmp/numbers'), and normal
     output is suppressed ('-o /dev/null').

'bbe -b ":525" -e "i 524 \x0a" -o /tmp/data_with_nl /tmp/data'
     A newline is added after every 525th byte of the file '/tmp/data'.
     Data with newlines is written to '/tmp/data_with_nl'.

'bbe -b ":526" -e "d 525 1" -o /tmp/data /tmp/data_with_nl'
     Every 526th byte (the newline inserted in the previous example) is
     removed from '/tmp/data_with_nl', restoring the original stream to
     '/tmp/data'.

'bbe -e "s/\x0d\x0a/\x0a/"'
     Converts DOS/Windows CRLF line terminators to Unix LF line
     terminators across a stream without altering any other binary data,
     equivalent to 'dos2unix'.

'bbe -e 's#http://old.example.com#https://new.example.com#' binary.elf'
     Analogous to 'sed' 's#old#new#', replaces a URL or file path inside
     an executable or binary file.  When the search or replacement
     string contains slashes ('/'), any non-conflicting character (such
     as '#' or ':') can be used as the delimiter.

'bbe -e "s#/usr/local/lib\x00#/opt/app/lib\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00#" binary.elf'
     When patching hardcoded paths or strings in compiled binaries, the
     replacement string should often maintain the exact byte length of
     the original to preserve binary offset tables.  Here, the shorter
     path is padded with null bytes ('\x00') to match the length of the
     original string.

'bbe -b 0:44 -e "D" input.wav > raw_pcm.bin'
     Analogous to 'sed' '1,10d' (deleting header lines), this command
     strips a fixed-size binary header.  The block is defined from
     offset 0 with a length of 44 bytes (the standard WAV file header),
     and 'D' deletes the entire block from the stream, leaving only the
     raw PCM audio payload.

'bbe -b "/\xff\xd8\xff/:/\xff\xd9/" -s firmware.bin > extracted_image.jpg'
     Analogous to 'sed -n '/START/,/END/p'', this command extracts an
     embedded payload between two binary delimiters.  With '-s'
     (suppress), all data outside the matching block is discarded, and
     only the block from the JPEG start marker ('0xff 0xd8 0xff') to the
     end marker ('0xff 0xd9') is printed.

'bbe -b "/\x7fELF/:4" -s -e "d 0 4;F h;A \n" disk.img'
     Analogous to 'sed -n '/pattern/='' (printing matching line
     numbers), this finds all occurrences of the ELF magic signature
     ('\x7fELF'), deletes the matched 4 bytes from output, and prints
     the exact stream offset in hexadecimal ('F h') followed by a
     newline.

'bbe -b 0x1000:8 -e "r 2 \x90\x90" program.bin'
     Analogous to patching a specific line in 'sed', this command
     replaces two bytes at offset 2 within the 8-byte block located at
     file offset '0x1000' with two x86 NOP instructions ('0x90 0x90'),
     effectively neutralizing a conditional check.

'bbe -b "/SECRET_KEY=/:27" -e "f 11 \x00" config.dat'
     Zeroes out sensitive data in a configuration file or memory dump.
     A 27-byte block starting with the string 'SECRET_KEY=' is located,
     and the fill command ('f 11 \x00') replaces all bytes from offset
     11 to the end of the block with null bytes ('0x00').

'bbe -e "y/\x00\xff/\xff\x00/" image.pbm'
     Analogous to character transliteration with 'sed' 'y/abc/xyz/',
     this command swaps all occurrences of '0x00' and '0xff' throughout
     the stream, inverting black and white pixels in a binary bitmap.

'bbe -b :1 -e "& \x7f" capture.bin'
     Clears the high bit (parity bit) from every byte in a serial
     communication capture, performing a bitwise AND with '0x7f' across
     each 1-byte block.

'bbe -b "/PK\x03\x04/:65536" -s -e "w zip_chunk_%03B.bin" -o /dev/null bundle.bin'
     Analogous to 'sed' 'w filename', this command demultiplexes or
     splits a binary stream into separate files.  For every ZIP file
     header found, a 64 KB block is extracted and saved into a
     sequentially numbered file ('zip_chunk_001.bin',
     'zip_chunk_002.bin', etc.)  using the '%B' block format specifier.

'bbe -b :16 -s -e "F h;p H;A \n" data.bin'
     Formats a binary file into a human-readable hex dump.  Each 16-byte
     block is printed with its stream offset in hexadecimal ('F h'),
     followed by the hexadecimal byte values ('p H') and a newline ('A
     \n').  To print both hexadecimal and ASCII side-by-side, use 'p HA'
     instead of 'p H'.


